-- Proof that somebody controls a mailbox, in flight.
--
-- One row is one six-digit code that has been mailed to an address and not yet
-- redeemed. Two features need exactly this — passwordless sign-in (LNL-74) and
-- verified address changes (LNL-71) — and they need it identically: the proofs
-- are the same, and only what the proof authorises differs. So the lifecycle is
-- one table and one store, parameterised by `purpose`, rather than two
-- near-copies of a security-sensitive flow. Two copies is how one of them
-- quietly ends up without the attempt cap.
--
-- ── Why this is on the volume and not in memory ────────────────────────────
--
-- The rate limiter that ships beside this is deliberately in memory, and the
-- reasoning does not carry across. A restart that forgets a rate-limit counter
-- costs an attacker a delay. A restart that forgets an issued code strands a
-- real user staring at a mail holding a code the server no longer recognises,
-- with nothing on screen that could explain why. That is a support ticket with
-- no diagnosis, produced by a deploy.
--
-- `oauth_auth_codes` already persists short-lived single-use secrets for exactly
-- this reason, and this table is deliberately built to its pattern rather than
-- inventing a parallel one.

CREATE TABLE email_codes (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,

    -- The mailbox the code was sent to, normalized by the caller before it gets
    -- here. Both callers trim and lowercase, so `Alice@X.com` and `alice@x.com`
    -- are one address and not two live codes — see normalizeEmail.
    address TEXT NOT NULL,

    -- EmailCodePurpose.name — SIGN_IN or EMAIL_CHANGE. Stored as the enum's name
    -- rather than its ordinal, for `users.provider`'s reason: an ordinal silently
    -- remaps if anyone reorders the enum, and the failure here would be a code
    -- issued to confirm an address becoming redeemable as a sign-in.
    --
    -- Part of the key, not decoration. That is the whole security property this
    -- column exists for: LNL-71 mails a code to an address the signed-in user has
    -- merely *claimed*, and if that code were redeemable at the sign-in endpoint,
    -- the confirmation mail would be a way into the account it was being attached
    -- to. Different purposes are different codes and never substitute.
    purpose TEXT NOT NULL,

    -- SHA-256 of the code, hex. Never the code.
    --
    -- The rule the OAuth tables already follow, and it applies here for the same
    -- reason: a database read must not hand over live credentials. Six digits is
    -- a small enough space that a hash is not much of a barrier to someone
    -- holding the table — but they would have to grind it per row, and the
    -- alternative is a column a `SELECT *` prints in plaintext.
    code_hash TEXT NOT NULL,

    -- How many wrong codes have been presented for this row.
    --
    -- Load-bearing, and the easiest thing in this design to leave out. Six digits
    -- is 10^6, which an unmetered script walks in minutes; the cap — not the
    -- length — is what makes a short code safe. At ATTEMPT_LIMIT the row is
    -- deleted rather than marked, so an exhausted code and an absent one are the
    -- same fact and cannot be told apart from outside.
    attempts INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,

    expires_at INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- Which account this proof was requested on behalf of, when the caller had
    -- one. Caller-supplied context that the primitive stores and hands back on
    -- redemption without ever interpreting.
    --
    -- Null for sign-in, and that is not an omission: at the moment a sign-in code
    -- is issued there is no account yet, and the address is the only thing known.
    -- Set for an address change, where the pending change belongs to the
    -- signed-in user who asked for it and the confirm step has to be able to
    -- check that it is still the same person.
    user_id INTEGER,

    -- At most one live code per address and purpose. Issuing a new one
    -- supersedes any outstanding one — see `issue`, which deletes first — so this
    -- is the constraint that makes "a stale code in an older mail cannot be used"
    -- a fact about the schema rather than about a store method being called
    -- correctly. Two purposes for one address still coexist, which is the point
    -- of having purpose in the key at all.
    UNIQUE (address, purpose),

    -- Deleting an account takes its pending address change with it. Without the
    -- cascade a deleted user could still have a code outstanding that named them.
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- At most one live code per account and purpose, alongside the per-address one
-- in the table.
--
-- Two different constraints answering two different questions, and both are
-- needed. The UNIQUE above stops one *address* accumulating codes; this stops one
-- *account* doing it. Without it, a user who typed `new1@` and then `new2@` would
-- have two pending changes and `pendingFor` — which asks "what is this user
-- waiting to confirm", singular — would find two rows and fail. That is not a
-- hypothetical: it is what happened the first time this was written without it.
--
-- Added by 20.sqm rather than 19.sqm, which created the table, because the rule
-- is not this primitive's. `user_id` here is opaque caller-supplied context, and
-- sign-in codes carry none at all; "one pending change per account" is LNL-71's
-- model arriving with LNL-71.
--
-- A partial index rather than a column constraint because `user_id` is nullable
-- and sign-in codes all carry NULL. SQLite treats NULLs as distinct in a UNIQUE
-- index, so `WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL` is belt to that brace and states the
-- intent besides. See 13.sqm for why a partial index is also the only way to add
-- uniqueness to an existing table here.
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX email_codes_one_per_user
    ON email_codes(user_id, purpose)
    WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL;

-- Drop any outstanding code for this address and purpose.
--
-- Called immediately before `insert` rather than relying on `INSERT OR REPLACE`,
-- so that superseding is a thing the code says it is doing. The UNIQUE above
-- makes forgetting it a constraint violation rather than a second live code.
deleteFor:
DELETE FROM email_codes WHERE address = ? AND purpose = ?;

insert:
INSERT INTO email_codes (address, purpose, code_hash, attempts, created_at, expires_at, user_id)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, 0, ?, ?, ?);

-- The live code for an address and purpose, if there is one.
--
-- Expiry is in the WHERE clause, not checked by the caller — the rule the OAuth
-- half of this schema follows, and it matters here for the reason
-- OAuthLoginState.sq gives: the sweep runs only at startup, so a server left up
-- for a month would otherwise honour a code that expired weeks ago. Sessions.kt
-- candidly documents that trap for itself; this table does not inherit it.
find:
SELECT id, address, purpose, code_hash, attempts, created_at, expires_at, user_id
FROM email_codes
WHERE address = ? AND purpose = ? AND expires_at > ?;

-- The address one user is currently waiting to confirm, if any.
--
-- What makes LNL-71's pending state survive the profile dialog being closed and
-- reopened: the pending change is a row here rather than a field in a view
-- model, so a user who closes the dialog to go and read their mail comes back to
-- find the request still waiting. Expiry is in the WHERE clause for `find`'s
-- reason.
--
-- Purpose is passed rather than hardcoded so this file stays free of any one
-- caller's vocabulary, but there is only one purpose a pending change can have.
findForUser:
SELECT id, address, purpose, code_hash, attempts, created_at, expires_at, user_id
FROM email_codes
WHERE user_id = ? AND purpose = ? AND expires_at > ?;

-- Give up on a pending change. The user's own "never mind", so that a mistyped
-- address is not a fifteen-minute wait — see the profile dialog's Cancel.
deleteForUser:
DELETE FROM email_codes WHERE user_id = ? AND purpose = ?;

-- One more wrong guess against this row.
recordAttempt:
UPDATE email_codes SET attempts = attempts + 1 WHERE id = ?;

-- Consumed, or given up on. Single use means the row goes either way: a
-- successful redemption and an exhausted attempt cap both end with no row, which
-- is what makes "reused" and "never existed" indistinguishable to the caller.
delete:
DELETE FROM email_codes WHERE id = ?;

deleteExpired:
DELETE FROM email_codes WHERE expires_at <= ?;

-- How many codes are outstanding. For the startup log, and for the tests that
-- assert a failed send left nothing behind — which is the one property here with
-- no observable consequence until somebody grinds six digits against a row that
-- should not exist.
countAll:
SELECT count(*) FROM email_codes;
